    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Deepen: choosing one or two words",
        "body": "Today we put the whole week into one skill: choosing. You are the guide. First listen to what the sentence is trying to say; then pick the path.\n\nAsk three questions in order. Is there a closed moment like «yesterday at five»? → usually ONE word (canté). Does the result still matter now («already», «this week»)? → often TWO words (he cantado). Is it background or past habit? → imperfect one word (cantaba). Ask them one after another until the sentence fits.\n\nNow hear the same verb in three little stories: «Terminé ayer» closes the past; «He terminado ya» keeps the result alive; «Leía cuando llamaron» paints a background and then a closed hit. Read them in a row and feel how the narrator’s focus changes.\n\nPráctica: Take five sentences and write both a one-word and a two-word version. Under each, justify the choice with one of the three questions — as if explaining to a classmate.\n\nError común: Mixing imperfect and preterite without asking whether the event closed. Pause and ask the question quietly before you write."
      }
    ],
    "summary": ""
  },
